<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Idi Amin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1925–<a href="/years/2003">2003</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Idi Amin was a Ugandan army officer who seized power from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milton-obote">Milton Obote</a> in 1971. He expelled Uganda's Asian community in 1972, and his regime killed tens of thousands of people. His invasion of the Kagera region of Tanzania in 1978 led to a Tanzanian counter-invasion that overthrew him in 1979, and he died in exile in Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/idi-amin.jpg" alt="Idi Amin" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gotfryd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Idi_Amin_at_UN_(United_Nations,_New_York)_gtfy.00132_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Idi Amin was born to a Kakwa father and a Lugbara mother. Virtually all retellings of his early life are contradictory, since he wrote no autobiography and never authorised an account of his life, and no records were kept for native Ugandans at the time. In 1940 he was rejected when he tried to register for primary school, reportedly because of his paternal Nubian heritage. He joined the King's African Rifles in 1946 as an assistant cook and received military training until 1947. He served in Kenya, was sent against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> rebels in 1949 and against the Mau Mau rebels in 1952, and was promoted to sergeant in 1953.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Uganda's independence in 1962 Amin was promoted to captain and then to major in 1963, and in 1964 he became Deputy Commander of the Army. A rift with President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milton-obote">Milton Obote</a> grew from Amin's support within the army, recruited from his home region of West Nile, and in October 1970 Obote took control of the armed forces. Having learned that Obote planned to arrest him for misappropriating army funds, Amin seized power on 25 January 1971 while Obote was at the Commonwealth summit in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, and he declared himself president on 2 February.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Amin ruled by decree, placed military tribunals above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> and replaced the General Service Unit with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-research-bureau">State Research Bureau</a>, whose headquarters in Nakasero became a scene of torture and capital punishment. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lango">Lango</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acholi">Acholi</a> soldiers were massacred in the barracks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinja">Jinja</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbarara">Mbarara</a> in July 1971, and bodies were often dumped in the River Nile. The killings continued throughout his eight years in power, and the exact number of dead is unknown, with estimates differing widely.</p><p>He expelled Asians, mostly Indian-Ugandans, in 1972, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> broke off diplomatic relations. In 1977 the United Kingdom broke off relations and he added CBE, for Conqueror of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>, to his titles. His attempt to annex Tanzania's Kagera Region in 1978 led President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a> to order an invasion, and Tanzanian and rebel forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampala">Kampala</a> in 1979. Amin fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, and finally to Saudi Arabia. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeddah">Jeddah</a> on 16 August 2003.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Idi Amin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In May 1966 troops under <b>Idi Amin</b> stormed the kabaka’s palace at Mengo, and Mutesa II fled to Britain, where he died in 1969.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-buganda">Kingdom of Buganda · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Idi Amin</b>’s Ugandan forces invaded and annexed the Kagera Salient in late 1978, Tanzania mobilised tens of thousands of soldiers and militia, drove the invaders out and, with Ugandan exile forces, advanced to capture Kampala on 11 April 1979, defeating Libyan troops sent to help Amin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tanzania">Tanzania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1972 <b>Idi Amin</b> ordered the expulsion of the Asian community, commonly estimated at around 80,000 people, and handed their businesses to supporters; together with the violence of his regime this ruined the economy, which shrank sharply through the 1970s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uganda">Uganda · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Most new rulers guarded their sovereignty, and the OAU’s rule of non-interference in members’ internal affairs kept it largely silent about coups, one-party rule and mass killings, such as those under <b>Idi Amin</b> in Uganda, and critics came to call it a “dictators’ club”.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>While President Milton Obote was abroad, army commander <b>Idi Amin</b> took power in a coup.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-25">25 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its early decades were marked by political upheaval, including the dictatorship of <b>Idi Amin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-9">9 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in King Faisal Specialist Hospital and Research Centre, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.67,46.68&amp;year=1975" aria-label="King Faisal Specialist Hospital and Research Centre on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5790 1255 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Idi Amin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6240 1525h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6240 1525h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=24.67,46.68&amp;year=1975">What was here in 1975? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
